1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,475
Complement looks like alphabet soup.

2
00:00:02,525 --> 00:00:04,861
But it follows a few simple rules.

3
00:00:04,911 --> 00:00:08,298
Learn them, and complement questions become easy.

4
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:11,433
Complement is a major effector mechanism of

5
00:00:11,483 --> 00:00:14,118
antibody immunity and of innate immunity.

6
00:00:14,168 --> 00:00:17,065
Its proteases circulate as inactive zymogens.

7
00:00:17,115 --> 00:00:21,191
A zymogen becomes active only when another protease cleaves it.

8
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:26,128
Each active protease cleaves many molecules of the next zymogen.

9
00:00:26,178 --> 00:00:28,866
So the signal amplifies at every step.

10
00:00:28,916 --> 00:00:31,316
Think of it like falling dominoes.

11
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:33,546
Two naming rules help.

12
00:00:33,596 --> 00:00:38,018
The larger fragment gets b, and the smaller gets a.

13
00:00:38,068 --> 00:00:42,402
C2 is the exception: the large C2a stays attached.

14
00:00:42,452 --> 00:00:46,348
And the numbers follow discovery, not action.

15
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:51,522
So the classical sequence is C1, C4, C2, C3, then C5 to C9.

16
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:53,929
Three pathways start complement.

17
00:00:53,979 --> 00:00:57,698
The classical pathway starts on antibody bound to antigen.

18
00:00:57,748 --> 00:01:01,661
The lectin pathway starts when lectins bind microbial sugars.

19
00:01:01,711 --> 00:01:06,209
The alternative pathway starts on microbial surfaces without antibody.

20
00:01:06,259 --> 00:01:09,848
Janeway notes that all three can start without antibody.

21
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,424
All three lead to one central event.

22
00:01:13,474 --> 00:01:17,426
A C3 convertase cleaves C3 into C3a and C3b.

23
00:01:17,476 --> 00:01:21,337
C3b binds covalently to the target surface.

24
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:28,977
If it finds no surface nearby, its thioester is hydrolyzed and C3b becomes inactive.

25
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:33,336
Host cells carry regulatory proteins that inhibit complement.

26
00:01:33,386 --> 00:01:34,598
Microbes lack them.

27
00:01:34,648 --> 00:01:38,385
So activation proceeds on microbes, not on our own cells.

28
00:01:38,767 --> 00:01:41,226
Now the classical pathway.

29
00:01:41,276 --> 00:01:46,727
C1q binds antibody, and C1r and C1s are serine proteases.

30
00:01:46,777 --> 00:01:52,229
C1q binds the CH2 domain of IgG or the CH3 domain of IgM.

31
00:01:52,279 --> 00:01:59,564
It must bind at least two heavy chains, so free antibody cannot activate C1.

32
00:01:59,933 --> 00:02:03,906
One antigen-bound IgM can bind two or more

33
00:02:03,956 --> 00:02:08,216
C1q, so IgM fixes complement better than IgG.

34
00:02:08,266 --> 00:02:15,974
IgG1 and IgG3 are efficient, IgG2 is weak, and IgG4 does not activate complement.

35
00:02:16,333 --> 00:02:21,420
C1q activates C1r, and C1r activates C1s.

36
00:02:21,470 --> 00:02:26,682
C1s cleaves C4, and C4b binds the surface.

37
00:02:26,732 --> 00:02:30,817
C2 binds C4b, and C1s cleaves it.

38
00:02:30,867 --> 00:02:36,956
C4b2a forms, the classical pathway C3 convertase.

39
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:42,159
The lectin pathway is the classical pathway's twin.

40
00:02:42,209 --> 00:02:48,565
Mannose-binding lectin, or MBL, and ficolins bind microbial sugars.

41
00:02:48,615 --> 00:02:52,771
MASP2 then cleaves C4 and C2, just like C1s.

42
00:02:52,821 --> 00:02:58,316
So the lectin pathway forms the same C3 convertase, C4b2a.

43
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:02,291
The alternative pathway never fully sleeps.

44
00:03:02,341 --> 00:03:07,931
Plasma C3 is cleaved spontaneously, about 1 to 2 percent per hour.

45
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:09,725
This is C3 tick-over.

46
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:15,023
The product, C3(H2O), binds factor B, and factor D cleaves it.

47
00:03:15,073 --> 00:03:18,953
A short-lived fluid-phase C3 convertase forms.

48
00:03:19,003 --> 00:03:26,046
Factor D is the only activating complement protease that circulates already active.

49
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:32,320
On a surface, C3b binds factor B, and factor D cleaves it.

50
00:03:32,370 --> 00:03:38,599
Bb stays, and C3bBb is the alternative pathway C3 convertase.

51
00:03:38,649 --> 00:03:44,466
Properdin stabilizes C3bBb, mainly on microbial surfaces.

52
00:03:44,516 --> 00:03:50,539
Note the twins: C3 is homologous to C4, and factor B to C2.

53
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:56,590
Each C3bBb makes more C3b, and each new C3b builds more C3bBb.

54
00:03:56,640 --> 00:04:01,867
So this loop amplifies complement started by any pathway.

55
00:04:01,917 --> 00:04:04,737
Think of it like a pilot light.

56
00:04:04,787 --> 00:04:10,199
On a bacterium, nothing blows it out, so it becomes a fire.

57
00:04:10,567 --> 00:04:17,579
Add one more C3b to a C3 convertase, and you get a C5 convertase.

58
00:04:17,629 --> 00:04:20,838
Classical and lectin: C4b2a3b.

59
00:04:20,888 --> 00:04:27,575
Alternative: one Bb with two C3b, which Janeway writes C3b2Bb.

60
00:04:27,625 --> 00:04:30,400
C2a or Bb then cleaves C5.

61
00:04:30,767 --> 00:04:35,225
C5b starts the membrane attack complex, or MAC.

62
00:04:35,275 --> 00:04:40,597
Unlike C3b, C5b does not bind covalently to the surface.

63
00:04:40,647 --> 00:04:46,065
C5b binds C6 and C7, and C5b-7 inserts into the membrane.

64
00:04:46,115 --> 00:04:49,038
C8 joins and makes small pores.

65
00:04:49,088 --> 00:04:53,835
Binding of the first C9 is the rate-limiting step.

66
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:58,331
More C9 molecules polymerize to finish the pore, C5b-9.

67
00:04:58,381 --> 00:05:02,588
Abbas says up to 18 C9 molecules; Janeway says 10 to 16.

68
00:05:02,638 --> 00:05:06,009
Water enters, and the cell swells and bursts.

69
00:05:06,059 --> 00:05:11,939
But lysis matters against only a few pathogens, such as thin-walled Neisseria.

70
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:19,919
The most important job of complement is to help phagocytes take up pathogens.

71
00:05:19,969 --> 00:05:24,301
C3b and C4b bind complement receptor 1, CR1.

72
00:05:24,351 --> 00:05:28,982
iC3b, a cleaved form of C3b, binds CR3 and CR4.

73
00:05:29,032 --> 00:05:33,564
CR1 alone triggers phagocytosis inefficiently.

74
00:05:33,614 --> 00:05:37,249
But iC3b on CR3 is enough on its own.

75
00:05:37,600 --> 00:05:38,697
A clinical link.

76
00:05:38,747 --> 00:05:42,497
IgM against capsules activates the classical pathway,

77
00:05:42,547 --> 00:05:46,153
and the spleen clears pneumococci and meningococci.

78
00:05:46,203 --> 00:05:49,307
So people without a spleen risk disseminated

79
00:05:49,357 --> 00:05:52,318
pneumococcal and meningococcal septicemia.

80
00:05:52,700 --> 00:06:00,891
The small fragments C3a, C4a and C5a degranulate mast cells and release histamine.

81
00:06:00,941 --> 00:06:03,705
We call them anaphylatoxins.

82
00:06:03,755 --> 00:06:06,016
C5a is the most potent.

83
00:06:06,066 --> 00:06:12,046
C3a is about 20-fold weaker, and C4a about 2500-fold weaker.

84
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:16,488
C3a and C5a can degranulate mast cells without IgE.

85
00:06:16,538 --> 00:06:21,763
C5a also attracts eosinophils and stimulates neutrophil motility.

86
00:06:21,813 --> 00:06:26,551
Large systemic amounts of anaphylatoxins cause a shock-like

87
00:06:26,601 --> 00:06:30,608
syndrome, similar to a systemic allergic reaction.

88
00:06:30,967 --> 00:06:34,085
Complement also helps adaptive immunity.

89
00:06:34,135 --> 00:06:40,421
CR1 on red cells carries immune complexes to the liver and spleen for clearance.

90
00:06:40,471 --> 00:06:46,045
And C3d on an antigen binds CR2 on B cells and helps B-cell activation.

91
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:47,624
Now the brakes.

92
00:06:47,674 --> 00:06:53,912
C3b cannot tell a host cell from a pathogen, so our cells need regulators.

93
00:06:53,962 --> 00:06:58,160
They act at three levels: C3 convertase formation,

94
00:06:58,210 --> 00:07:01,559
the convertases themselves, and the MAC.

95
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:06,362
At the start sits C1 inhibitor, or C1-INH, a serpin.

96
00:07:06,412 --> 00:07:11,357
It removes C1r and C1s from C1q, and it inactivates MASP2.

97
00:07:11,407 --> 00:07:18,161
It also inhibits kallikrein and factor XII, which promote bradykinin formation.

98
00:07:18,211 --> 00:07:22,208
C1-INH deficiency causes hereditary angioedema.

99
00:07:22,567 --> 00:07:29,472
At the convertases, decay accelerators pull the enzyme apart.

100
00:07:29,522 --> 00:07:34,717
DAF, or CD55, dissociates both C3 convertases.

101
00:07:34,767 --> 00:07:41,444
Factor H displaces Bb, and C4-binding protein displaces C2.

102
00:07:41,494 --> 00:07:44,295
Factor I is the scissors.

103
00:07:44,345 --> 00:07:53,759
It cuts C3b and C4b only with a cofactor: factor H, MCP, C4-binding protein or CR1.

104
00:07:54,133 --> 00:08:02,553
At the MAC, CD59, or protectin, joins the complex after C5b-8 and blocks C9.

105
00:08:02,603 --> 00:08:10,354
In plasma, S protein binds soluble C5b-7 and protects bystander cells.

106
00:08:10,404 --> 00:08:15,257
Loss of GPI anchors removes DAF and CD59 and

107
00:08:15,307 --> 00:08:20,049
causes paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.

108
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,685
Five things to remember.

109
00:08:22,735 --> 00:08:26,966
One: three pathways converge on C3 cleavage.

110
00:08:27,016 --> 00:08:34,555
Two: classical and lectin make C4b2a, and the alternative pathway makes C3bBb.

111
00:08:34,605 --> 00:08:39,323
Three: the alternative loop amplifies everything.

112
00:08:39,373 --> 00:08:43,312
Four: C3b opsonizes, C5a is the strongest

113
00:08:43,362 --> 00:08:47,496
anaphylatoxin, and the MAC lyses Neisseria.

114
00:08:47,546 --> 00:08:51,096
Five: regulators act at three levels.

115
00:08:51,467 --> 00:08:52,599
One exam trap.

116
00:08:52,649 --> 00:08:56,651
Factor H regulates only the alternative pathway.

117
00:08:56,701 --> 00:08:59,521
It does not displace C2a from C4b.

118
00:08:59,571 --> 00:09:03,489
The plasma brake for C4b is C4-binding protein.

119
00:09:03,539 --> 00:09:08,217
Next: complement deficiencies and hereditary angioedema.
